}

part_reading() {
  heading $1
  say "Now the same change by symbol: what gdm opens on this branch, and what enter opens on an MR in gmr. Things to try, in order:"
  look 1 "The top row is the only entry point, handleReturn. The footer says how many symbols there are and how many to start from."
  look 2 "${a}~${r} at the start of a row: its signature changed, so whatever calls it is worth reading again. No mark: only its body did, and nothing outside it can break. Then the lines it added and removed (+5 alone is new), and ${k}▸ 3${r}: three changed symbols under it."
  look 3 "${k}l${r} opens a row to the changed symbols it uses, in the order it uses them: refundAmount, receiptLine, and restockingFee, which it now calls itself. Keep going, down to daysSince. ${k}h${r} closes, from the row or anything under it; ${k}L${r} and ${k}H${r} open and close everything."
  look 4 "${k}⇢${r} before a row: the mention is new in this change. That is rewiring, as against behavior, and worth knowing apart."
  look 5 "The preview is the whole symbol as the change leaves it, real line numbers, changed lines marked. Changed symbols it uses are in yellow; ${k}p${r} adds a dim ↳ line under the changed line that first uses each, saying what it is. ${k}i${r} shows all of those whole instead. ${k}]${r} and ${k}[${r} jump the preview from change to change."
  look 6 "${k}enter${r} opens your editor on the symbol, on the hunk in view; nvim opens it in a diff against the base. Quitting comes back."
  if (( $+commands[claude] )); then
    look 7 "Meanwhile Claude Haiku glosses it: a few words on what each symbol does, the change aside, heading its preview, and a few on what the change does to it, on the diff's header. Nothing moves under you, so the rest shows the next time it opens: the entry points in the order to read them, with why, and one line at the top on what the change introduces. It sees only the symbol table and each symbol's diff, has no tools, and posts nothing."
  else
    look 7 "With Claude Code installed, Haiku also glosses each row in a few words and says which entry point to read first. It is not installed here, so the rows stay bare."
  fi
  say "${k}?${r} in the picker lists every key it has."
  demo_reset
  launch gdm
}

part_radius() {
  heading $1
  say "What the diff could not show you. Open the tree again (${k}L${r} opens it all):"
  look 1 "Under refundAmount, a dim row: its callers elsewhere, the code the change did not touch that uses it. tsgo found them. ${k}l${r} opens it to each caller as a signature, by package."
  look 2 "exchangeCredit carries ${b}✗${r}: the caller was type checked with the changed files as they were, then as the change leaves them, and gained an error. An error it had already is not held against the change."
  look 3 "Its preview is the caller whole, the calls underlined, and the error under its line. ${k}enter${r} opens it on the error."
  if (( $+commands[claude] )); then
    look 4 "Claude Sonnet looks at every broken caller in the background. Under the error goes its read of it: whether the fix belongs in the caller or in the change, and, when it is sure of one, the edit as a diff. It takes a minute; the footer says while it works. ${k}F${r} makes the edit in this checkout, uncommitted, and tsgo checks it again: the ${b}✗${r} turns ${g}✓${r}, fixed in your checkout. The demo is thrown away, so go ahead."
  else
    look 4 "With Claude Code installed, Sonnet proposes a fix under each error, and ${k}F${r} makes it in the checkout, where tsgo checks it again."
  fi
  (( $+commands[tsgo] && $+commands[node] )) ||
    say "${a}tsgo or node is not installed here, so there is no callers row.${r} brew install tsgo (and node) to see it; the rest of the tour works without."
  say "This is the reason to review by symbol even when the diff looks fine: a reviewer reading files has no way to see this row, and it is the one that pages someone."
  demo_reset
  launch gdm
}

